---
// REVIEW: Layer 2 interpretation for the hEDS 2017 criteria checklist,
// drafted for Craig's review. Static, always visible, rendered at build time.
// FAQ answers come from heds-criteria.content.ts and are shown word for word
// (they also feed the page's FAQPage JSON-LD).
import { FAQ, SOURCES } from '../../tools/heds-criteria.content';
import '../../styles/interpretation.css';

const s = SOURCES;
---

<!-- REVIEW -->
<section class="interpretation" aria-labelledby="understanding-heds">
	<h2 id="understanding-heds">Understanding your hEDS criteria checklist result</h2>

	<h3 id="what-it-measures">What the score measures</h3>
	<p>
		There is no score: the checklist records which of the three 2017 criteria your answers meet
		(<a href={s.malfait.href} rel="noopener">{s.malfait.label}</a>;
		<a href={s.checklist.href} rel="noopener">{s.checklist.label}</a>).
	</p>
	<p>{FAQ.criteria.a}</p>

	<h3 id="score-bands">Score bands</h3>
	<p>
		Each criterion shows as <strong>met</strong>, <strong>not met</strong> or <strong>needs a clinician</strong>, meaning it
		depends on something unknown, such as a sign needing examination or an exclusion not yet assessed. Criterion 3 will
		usually show this, because only a clinician can confirm the exclusions, and the diagnosis is theirs to make.
	</p>
	<p>{FAQ.hsd.a}</p>

	<h3 id="change">What a change in score means</h3>
	<p>
		Your result should change only when your answers do, for example after an examination confirms a Feature A sign. The
		criteria will change too: new criteria for all types of EDS and for HSD are due on 1 December 2026
		(<a href={s.criteria2026.href} rel="noopener">{s.criteria2026.label}</a>).
	</p>

	<h3 id="what-it-does-not-measure">What it does not measure</h3>
	<p>{FAQ.pots.a}</p>
	<p>
		For those symptoms, see the <a href="/tools/compass-31/">COMPASS-31</a> and the
		<a href="/tools/nasa-lean-test/">NASA lean test</a>.
	</p>

	<h3 id="taking-it-to-your-gp">Taking this to your GP or consultant</h3>
	<p>{FAQ.self.a}</p>
	<p>
		Bring the copied summary with its date, your Beighton score, the Feature A signs marked present or unknown, any family
		diagnoses, and a short history of pain and dislocations. Ask which unknowns can be checked and who should assess the
		exclusions, often a rheumatologist or clinical geneticist. See also
		<a href="/nhs/preparing-for-gp-appointment/">preparing for a GP appointment</a>.
	</p>

	<h3 id="sources">Sources</h3>
	<ul class="interp-sources">
		<li>Malfait F, Francomano C, Byers P, et al. The 2017 international classification of the Ehlers-Danlos syndromes. <em>Am J Med Genet C Semin Med Genet</em> 2017;175(1):8–26. <a href={s.malfait.href} rel="noopener">DOI</a></li>
		<li>The Ehlers-Danlos Society. Diagnostic criteria for hypermobile Ehlers-Danlos syndrome (hEDS) checklist. <a href={s.checklist.href} rel="noopener">Checklist</a></li>
		<li>Castori M, Tinkle B, Levy H, Grahame R, Malfait F, Hakim A. A framework for the classification of joint hypermobility and related conditions. <em>Am J Med Genet C Semin Med Genet</em> 2017;175(1):148–157. <a href={s.castori.href} rel="noopener">DOI</a></li>
		<li>The Ehlers-Danlos Society. What is HSD? <a href={s.hsd.href} rel="noopener">Web page</a></li>
		<li>The Ehlers-Danlos Society. New global diagnostic criteria for EDS and HSD, 2026. <a href={s.criteria2026.href} rel="noopener">News item</a></li>
	</ul>
</section>
